> **Final Narrative Report: Achievements and Progress**
> 
> **Project Overview:**
> 
> The project, funded by the Gates Foundation, aimed to deliver a global review and analytical synthesis of the macroeconomic and fiscal impacts of inaction on climate mitigation and adaptation. Our objective was to provide evidence-based insights and develop a case for investing in adaptation, particularly for economic decision-makers such as Ministries of Finance (MoFs), in preparation for COP30 in Belém, Brazil.
> 
> **Achievements:**
> 
> 1. **Synthesis of Literature and Evidence:**
>     - Conducted a comprehensive synthesis of global literature covering the drivers of macroeconomic impacts from climate change, costs of inaction, and the fiscal implications for public finances. This synthesis highlighted gaps in adaptation financing and provided a robust critique of the methodologies used in existing studies.
> 
> 2. **Engagement with Finance Ministries:**
>     - Engaged with [number] of Finance Ministries and economic decision-makers across [number] countries through targeted interviews and consultations. This engagement helped us understand the practical needs and constraints faced by MoFs in integrating adaptation into fiscal policies.
> 
> 3. **Case Studies and Real-World Examples:**
>     - Completed [number] case studies that illustrated the economic and fiscal outcomes of countries that have invested in adaptation versus those that have not. These case studies included analysis from the UK, Rwanda, and other countries [specify others], providing inspirational examples of successful adaptation strategies.
> 
> 4. **Development of Investment Framework:**
>     - Developed an investment framework for adaptation that includes real-world fiscal planning and investment strategies. This framework serves as a guideline for MoFs to incorporate adaptation into their economic models and decision-making processes.
> 
> **Progress Towards Desired Outcomes:**
> 
> 1. **Improved Awareness and Understanding:**
>     - Increased awareness among key economic decision-makers of the macroeconomic costs of climate inaction and the potential benefits of timely adaptation investments. Our findings were framed in a solution-oriented manner, enhancing their accessibility and impact on policy discussions.
> 
> 2. **Policy and Fiscal Integration:**
>     - Contributed to the integration of adaptation into fiscal strategies through participation in existing processes such as the Coalition of Finance Ministers for Climate Action. Our collaboration with SystemIQ and other organizations ensured alignment with other COP30-related initiatives.
> 
> 3. **Identification of Key Research Gaps:**
>     - Identified critical gaps in the existing literature and analysis, particularly concerning the quantification of adaptation costs and benefits. These insights have been documented to guide future research efforts.
